  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{\u2033}+4y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={sin}^{2}\u2061x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+B{sin}^{2}\u2061x.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{\u2032}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\u2033}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+4(\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x)=0.\\,\u2713\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032}(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033}(x) & =-2{sin}^{2}\u2061x+2{cos}^{2}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-2{sin}^{2}\u2061x+2{cos}^{2}\u2061x+4({sin}^{2}\u2061x)=2{sin}^{2}\u2061x+2{cos}^{2}\u2061x=2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is false.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution while ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+B{sin}^{2}\u2061x$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false.",
